Food Anaphylaxis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Sudden urticaria/angioedema, Wheezing/stridor, Rapid hypotension/shock

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-022
Reason for Visit: Acute, precipitous onset of generalized hives, facial swelling, and respiratory distress immediately following food ingestion requiring emergent evaluation for anaphylactic shock.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a child experiencing severe throat tightness, wheezing, and facial swelling minutes after eating a new food. Immediate, critical intervention was mandated to assess airway patency, definitively diagnose anaphylaxis, and administer life-saving intramuscular adrenaline prior to the next in-hours period. Delaying assessment would predictably result in fatal cardiopulmonary arrest.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He ate a peanut butter cracker, and within minutes his face swelled up, he broke out in hives, and now he is coughing and grabbing his throat."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a sudden, acute onset of symptoms strictly occurring within minutes following the ingestion of a specific allergenic food. The parents describe rapidly progressive, generalized cutaneous signs prominently featuring diffuse urticaria, intense pruritus, and marked flushing. Angioedema explicitly involving the lips and periorbital soft tissues is visually obvious. There is acute respiratory compromise characterized directly by audible wheezing and a persistent severe cough. The child reports a subjective, terrifying sensation of impending doom and distinct throat tightness. There is severe gastrointestinal involvement featuring sudden crampy abdominal pain and one episode of forceful vomiting. The presentation completely lacks any preceding infectious prodrome.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, actively struggling to breathe, panicking.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of asphyxiation and cardiovascular collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 80/45 | HR: 165 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 38 | SpO2: 90% | Wt: 20 Kg
General: Profoundly unwell, flushed, visibly panicked, audible wheeze at rest.
Physical Exam: Cutaneous examination reveals diffuse, raised, erythematous urticarial plaques across the torso and extremities. Massive angioedema of the lips and bilateral eyelids is present. Respiratory examination reveals severe tachypnea, prominent intercostal retractions, and loud, diffuse expiratory wheezing across all lung fields. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates severe tachycardia progressing rapidly to hemodynamic instability with profound hypotension. The diagnostic tetrad of sudden urticaria/angioedema, wheezing/stridor, and rapid hypotension/shock is definitively confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Half-eaten peanut snack visible on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral antihistamine administered by parents 10 minutes ago with zero clinical improvement.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Food Anaphylaxis - Status: Critical Emergency (Active anaphylactic shock)
Differentials:

Acute Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out by the simultaneous presence of generalized urticaria, facial angioedema, and severe hypotension.

Acute Viral Urticaria - Ruled out explicitly by the rapid onset immediately following specific food ingestion, presence of respiratory compromise, and cardiovascular collapse.

Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out by the presence of severe cutaneous findings (hives/swelling) and gastrointestinal symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical (Immediate threat to life)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's clinical presentation constituted an absolute, time-critical medical emergency. The sudden onset of multisystem involvement (cutaneous, respiratory, and cardiovascular) immediately following allergen exposure mandated a definitive diagnosis of anaphylaxis. Telephone triage correctly prioritized this as a highest-tier emergency. Objective assessment revealed borderline shock and impending airway closure. Delaying care or attempting private transport would have likely resulted in fatal asphyxiation or cardiovascular collapse. Immediate, on-site administration of intramuscular adrenaline was universally mandated and directly achieved a rapid, life-saving resolution of systemic shock, stabilizing the patient for urgent ambulance transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Adrenaline (Epinephrine) 1:1000, 0.20 mg strictly into the anterolateral thigh stat. Activated 000 for immediate priority ambulance dispatch. Positioned the patient supine with elevated lower extremities to maximize venous return.
Positive Behavioral Change: The parents must actively verbally praise the child for keeping their legs elevated and remaining still while waiting for the ambulance. Rewarding the child's physical stillness with calm, continuous verbal reassurance directly reduces their panic, lowering the heart rate and oxygen demand while the adrenaline takes effect.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Adrenaline autoinjector (EpiPen) prescription to be issued at the hospital.
Follow-up: Handover to intensive care retrieval team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate handover to Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance). Do not transport by private car under any circumstances due to the high risk of a biphasic anaphylactic reaction.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a life-threatening critical emergency. Symptoms may rebound (biphasic reaction) even after adrenaline administration.
Clinical Course: The adrenaline will rapidly improve breathing and blood pressure, but the child requires a minimum of 4 hours of strict hospital observation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Wait precisely in the current position for the ambulance. Do not allow the child to stand or walk, as this can trigger immediate fatal cardiac arrest.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The child's immune system massively overreacted to the food protein, causing the throat to swell shut and the blood pressure to crash. The adrenaline injection is the only medication that stops this reaction; antihistamines do not save lives in anaphylaxis.
Actionable Steps: Keep the child lying perfectly flat with legs up. Never allow them to sit up suddenly. Once discharged from the hospital, carry two adrenaline autoinjectors at all times.

